  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 527 AM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nOne last dry day before chances for showers and storms move into the \narea for the weekend (50-90 percent chances), and persist on and off \nthrough next week (20-40 percent). Expect increasing clouds today \nwith a low chance far west of a few showers towards the evening \nhours. Expect rain chances to increase and spread eastward through \nthe night, with the best potential west of I 69. More widespread \nrain showers and scattered storms persist Saturday (70-90 percent \nchance). Additionally, SPC issued a marginal risk for isolated \nstrong/severe storms Saturday afternoon, mainly including a small \nportion of our Indiana counties along/south of IN-218/IN-18 (Wells, \nGrant, Blackford, Jay, and Adams Counties). The primary threat with \nany stronger storms would be damaging winds up to 60 mph. Otherwise, \nthe main hazards with storms will be gusty winds, lightning, and \nheavy rain. Localized flooding could occur in areas that see higher \nrates, depending on how fast the system moves/moisture convergence \nfocuses. The marginal risk is associated with a more narrow \naxis of sfc instability (300-800 J/kg sfc cape) that reaches \ninto the aforementioned area, with decent moisture transport \n(Td's in upper 60s, low 70s) and 0-6 km bulk shear ranging from \n30-50 knots (per most guidance, the NAM12 has 50-60kts but \ncloser to NE IN/NW OH...as it is further north than the other \nguidance). Don't expect any widespread severe weather given \npaltry mid level lapse rates at this point.\n\nTemperatures will be in the 80s today, then fall into the 70s, low \n80s for Saturday into Monday. Temperatures rise into the low-mid 80s \nonce again Tuesday into Friday. \n\nOur upper level ridge and subsequent surface high will drift \neastward today, with a broad upper level trough/closed low dropping \ninto the IA/IL/IN/KY area today into Sunday, where it meanders \nslowly east (or slightly northeast) from there. There is still a \nbit of a difference in the placement of the upper trough/vort \nmax amongst guidance. Because of this, the surface low is placed\ndifferently, with discrepancies really showing up by 00z Sun. \nSome of the guidance takes the surface low further south \n(centered near Indy/KY) whereas the NAM shifts the low more \nnortheast into Lake Erie/Southern Ontario. With the blocking \npattern establishing itself through Monday as mentioned in the \nprevious discussion, the trough may linger longer-especially \nthrough Sunday, but could be as late as Mon/Tue. Increased pops\nsubstantially Sunday to account for this slower movement, with \nthe best potential in the morning along/south of US 24 (55-85 \npercent). Elsewhere, chances range from 20-50 percent Sunday.\n\nThere is a moderate swim risk for Lake Michigan beaches along both\nLa Porte County, IN and Southern Berrien County, MI on \nSaturday, with easterly winds strengthening and shifting to the \nnortheast at 15 to 25 knots later in the day. Waves will build \nup to around 3 feet by late afternoon, greatest near New \nBuffalo, MI to Michigan City, IN. \n\nThere is a high swim risk for all beaches in Berrien and La Porte \nCounties on Sunday. Northeast winds will be around 15 to 25 knots \nwith gusts up to 30 knots, shifting to the north Sunday afternoon. \nWaves continue to build from 3 to 5 feet Saturday evening to \naround 4 to 7 feet by Sunday afternoon-with the highest waves \nfrom Warren Dunes to Michigan City, IN. Expect a strong \nsouthward flowing longshore current and strong structural \ncurrents, especially on the north sides of piers and breakwalls.\nRip currents are expected. Those planning to visit the beaches \nshould stay out of the water. \n\nIssued a small craft advisory from 18z Saturday into 9z Monday for \nboth marine zones. For now, held off on issuing a beach hazards \nstatement-prefer to wait until the next wave model run this \nafternoon to narrow down timing/increase confidence given it's still \nbeyond 24 hours.",
